.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt ARNO 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm arno
.Nd a harness for language models in the terminal
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Ar prompt ...
.Nm
.Fl \-resume Ar id prompt ...
.Nm
.Fl \-serve
.Nm
.Fl \-attach
.Op Ar pid | Fl l
.Nm
.Fl h | \-help
.Nm
.Fl \-version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
puts a language model in a terminal next to your code.
You steer.
The model reads, searches and proposes edits through a few tools, and
every edit waits for your approval.
.Nm
is written in raz and is a rebuild of an older arno written in Pascal;
it reads that arno's configuration and saved conversations as they are.
.Pp
With no arguments on a terminal,
.Nm
takes the full screen: the transcript above, a rule that shows what is
going on or a question, then the input and a status line.
The status line shows the mode, the safety, the model and its
provider, the context in use, tokens and cost, and the conversation's
title.
See
.Sx THE SCREEN .
.Pp
With a
.Ar prompt ,
or when standard input or output is not a terminal,
.Nm
prints plain lines instead.
The prompt, its words joined by blanks, is one turn of a new
conversation, and
.Nm
exits.
With no prompt and no terminal, each line of standard input is a
message to the model, or a command such as
.Ic /help ;
the end of the input, or
.Ic /quit ,
ends the session.
See
.Sx PLAIN MODE .
.Pp
.Fl \-resume Ar id
makes a saved conversation the current one before the prompt is sent, so
that a script can go on where a run stopped, for instance one that used
up
.Cm max_steps ,
with its history, its scratch directory and the provider's prompt
cache.
.Ar id
may be an id, a number from
.Ic /list
.Pq 1 is the conversation changed last
or a word from a title, as for
.Ic /load .
.Pp
Every turn is saved, however it ends, so a conversation can be taken up
again later.
Requests go straight to the provider's API, over HTTPS (HTTP for a local
Ollama); the key is read from an environment variable and sent only to
the provider.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--attach [pid | -l]"
.It Ar prompt ...
One turn of a new conversation, then exit.
An empty prompt is refused, with status 2, as a script's prompt file
never written is a mistake and not a question.
.It Fl \-resume Ar id prompt ...
One more turn of the saved conversation
.Ar id .
An unknown one is refused, with status 1.
.It Fl \-serve
Run one session for the program that started
.Nm ,
speaking its protocol on standard input and output.
See
.Sx SERVE
and
.Xr arno-serve 5 .
It takes no other argument.
.It Fl \-attach Op Ar pid
Show another arno's session in this terminal, and type into it.
Needs a terminal.
See
.Sx ATTACH .
.It Fl \-attach Fl l
List the sessions that can be attached to: process id, owner, project
root and title, a line each, parted by tabs; or
.Ql "no sessions" .
.It Fl h , \-help
Print the usage and exit.
Only as the first argument; anywhere else it is a word of the prompt.
.It Fl \-version
Print the version, as the release is tagged, and exit.
Only as the first argument, as for
.Fl \-help .
.El
.Sh THE SCREEN
The screen shows, from the top, the transcript, a rule, the input
and the status line.
Your messages, the model's, tool calls, notes and errors are told
apart by colour.
A tool call is one line until opened.
The rule says what is going on: the model working, a question waiting
for your key, or copy mode.
.Pp
The status line starts with the mode, its name on a colour of its own:
chat blue, review green, improve cyan, pair in the text's own colour,
build yellow.
Beside it is the safety, a green
.Ql \[u25CF] auto
or a yellow
.Ql \[u25C6] safe ,
and in build mode the plan,
.Ql plan \(OK
once you approved it,
.Ql "plan ?"
before.
Then come the model and its provider, the context as a bar of eight
cells and a percentage, green, yellow from 60%, red from 85%, the
tokens and cost, and the title, cut to the room left.
On a narrow terminal the hints go first, then the title, the tokens
and cost, the provider, the context and the model; the mode, safety
and plan stay.
A change of mode or safety is said on the rule for two seconds.
.Pp
A message is a turn, run on a thread of its own, so the screen stays
live while the model works.
A turn stops at the next read of the reply after you say so, not in
the middle of a wait for the server.
.Pp
The keys are emacs's.
The input is edited with the usual emacs keys for moving, deleting,
killing and yanking, and grows to eight rows as you type.
.Bl -tag -width "PgUp / PgDn" -compact
.It RET
Send.
.It M-RET
A new line in the input.
Pasted text keeps its newlines.
.It C-c
Stop the turn; with none running, empty the input.
.It C-d
Quit, when the input is empty.
.It C-z
Stop
.Nm
as the shell stops a job, the terminal given back, wherever it is;
.Ic fg
brings it back, drawn whole.
.It S-Tab
The next mode; see
.Sx MODES .
.It TAB
Open or close the newest tool call.
.It C-o
Open or close every tool call.
.It PgUp , PgDn
Scroll the transcript by a page.
.It Up , Down , S-Up , S-Down
Scroll by a line, as the wheel does; Up and Down move between the
input's lines first.
.It C-l
Redraw.
.It C-p
Recall the earlier input, on the input's first line.
What you were typing waits at the bottom.
.It C-n
Recall the later input, on its last line.
.It M-p , M-n
The earlier or later input that starts as the input does, up to the
cursor.
.It C-r
Search earlier input, newest first, ignoring case: type to narrow, C-r
again for an older match, Backspace to take a character off, C-g or Esc
to put the input back as it was.
Any other key keeps the match and does its usual work.
.It C-x C-e
Edit the input in
.Ev VISUAL ,
else
.Ev EDITOR ,
else
.Xr vi 1 ;
what you save comes back into the input.
Under the input the file shows the last exchange, below the scissors
line of
.Ic git commit -v ,
.Ql "# ------------------------ >8 ------------------------" :
that line and all below it are dropped.
Not while a turn runs.
.It C-v
Attach the image on the clipboard.
Once
.Xr xclip 1
has found a PNG there, it is saved and an
.Ql @
reference to the file is typed at the cursor; otherwise the rule says
why not.
.It M-v
Copy mode; see
.Sx COPY MODE .
.El
.Pp
Input is kept across runs, in
.Pa ~/.local/share/arno/history .
.Pp
While a command or an
.Ql @path
is typed, what it may become is shown over the foot of the transcript:
C-n, C-p and the arrows choose, TAB or RET takes the one chosen, and Esc
hides the menu until the input changes.
.Pp
A question, a path outside the project, an edit, a program to run, takes
over the rule: one of its keys answers.
C-c, C-d, Esc or RET refuses; C-c stops the turn too.
The wheel, <up> and <down>, Page Up and Page Down scroll the transcript
all the while, and M-v enters copy mode, to read a long plan or diff
before answering; the question is back when copy mode ends.
After a no to a change,
.Nm
asks
.Ql "why not?" :
RET gives the line you typed, and C-g or Esc leaves it empty.
What you type goes to the model with the refusal, as
.Ql "declined by user: keep the old name" ,
so that it can try again knowing what was wrong.
.Pp
The mouse is not captured.
.Sh COPY MODE
Copy mode puts a cursor on the transcript, much as
.Xr tmux 1
does over a pane's scrollback, to move, search, select and copy.
Enter it with M-v.
New text arriving below does not move the view.
.Pp
Copying sets the system clipboard through the terminal with the OSC 52
escape sequence, so it needs no helper program and works over
.Xr ssh 1 ;
a terminal that does not know it ignores it.
A copied paragraph comes out as the one line it was, not as the rows it
was wrapped into.
Copying with nothing selected copies the cursor's line.
.Pp
The keys are emacs's, with vi's letters where emacs has none:
.Bl -tag -width "C-SPC, v ; V" -compact
.It C-b C-f C-p C-n
Left, right, up, down; so do the arrows and h l k j.
.It M-f , w
A word on.
.It M-b , b
A word back.
.It e
To the end of the word.
.It C-a , 0 , Home
Start of the row.
.It C-e , $ , End
End of the row.
.It M-m , ^
First non-blank of the row.
.It M-< , g
First row.
.It M-> , G
Last row.
.It M-v , PgUp
A page back.
.It C-v , PgDn
A page on.
.It C-u , C-d
Half a page back, on.
.It M-{ , {
The paragraph before.
.It M-} , }
The paragraph after.
.It C-SPC , v
Select characters; V selects rows.
.It C-x C-x , o
Move the cursor to the selection's other end.
.It RET , y , M-w , C-w
Copy, and leave.
.It C-s , /
Search on; C-r or ? searches back; n again, N again the other way.
.It C-g , Esc
Drop the selection; with none, leave.
.It q , C-c
Leave.
.El
.Pp
A word is a run of letters, digits and underscores, or of other marks.
A search takes its text literally, ignores case, and goes round the ends
of the transcript.
Paragraph motions stop at blank rows, which fall between messages.
.Sh PLAIN MODE
In plain mode the model's text goes to standard output as it arrives.
Everything else goes to standard error, a line each: each tool call as
.Ql "\[u25B8] name arguments"
and what came of it, a failure's first line or how many lines the result
had; notes
.Nm
makes, as
.Ql "arno: ..." ;
and questions.
A command's text goes to standard output, an error to standard error.
.Pp
A question is put on standard error, and answered by a line of standard
input: the first byte of
.Ql y ,
.Ql n
or, where it is offered,
.Ql a
for always.
Anything else, and the end of the input, is no.
After a no to a change the next line says why, and an empty line says
nothing.
With standard input a terminal, a
.Ql "> "
asks for each line.
.Pp
^C stops a turn, keeping what came, not the session.
When a prompt on the command line has run,
.Nm
says on standard error
.Ql "arno --resume ID PROMPT goes on from here" .
.Pp
In one-shot mode the stage's question (see
.Sx THE STAGE )
is answered the same way; if there is no one to answer, the diff is left
in the scratch directory as
.Pa stage.diff .
.Sh COMMANDS
Lines that start with a slash are commands for
.Nm ,
never sent to the model.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic /help
Commands and modes.
.It Ic /new
Start a new conversation, with an empty scratch directory.
Drops the path grants and the declined changes.
.It Ic /clear
Trim the history to the first exchange, drop the path grants, forget the
declined changes and empty the scratch directory.
.It Ic /compact Op Ar focus
Ask the model to summarize the conversation, then continue from that
summary alone, as a new conversation titled after the old one with
.Dq (compacted)
appended.
The full history stays saved under its old id; see
.Ic /list .
With
.Ar focus ,
the summary pays particular attention to it.
Path grants are kept.
If the request fails or is interrupted, nothing changes.
.It Ic /list Op Ar word
Recent conversations, the one changed last first, numbered, up to 20,
filtered by title.
A conversation not given a name with
.Ic /rename
is titled by the start of its first message.
.It Ic /load Ar n | id | word
Resume a conversation: a number from
.Ic /list ,
an id, or the one changed last whose title contains
.Ar word .
When the session is staged, the work the conversation left unapplied
comes back into the copy; see
.Sx THE STAGE .
.It Ic /export Op Ar n | id
Print a conversation, by default this one, as JSON.
.It Ic /rename Ar title
Name this conversation; with no title, clear the name.
.It Ic /system Op Ar text | Cm \-
Without an argument, show this conversation's own system prompt, or,
when it has none,
.Nm Ns 's
own instructions.
With
.Ar text ,
make that the conversation's own; with
.Cm \- ,
go back to
.Nm Ns 's
own.
It is saved with the conversation and sent from the next request on.
It takes the place of the instructions alone.
What follows them stays: the working directory, the context files and
project map, the scratch directory, the notes, the facts remembered, what
.Nm
says of itself, and the mode.
.It Ic /mode Op Ar name
Show or set the mode; see
.Sx MODES .
.It Ic /budget Op Ar dollars
Show or set, for this session, the dollars at list price a turn may
spend, workers included:
.Cm build_budget
in
.Cm build
mode,
.Cm turn_budget
in the others.
0 is no limit.
.It Ic /model Op Ar name
Show or set the model.
.It Ic /models
List model names for the current provider, for
.Ic /model
to set: asked of the provider's server, else
.Nm Ns 's
own list.
.It Ic /provider Op Ar name
Show or set the provider; see
.Sx PROVIDERS .
Setting one drops the request parameters the configuration gave.
.It Ic /tools
List the tools on offer in this mode.
.It Ic /mcp Op Cm login Ar NAME
What became of each MCP server: the tools it offers, or why it was not
attached.
With
.Cm login ,
log in to the server
.Ar NAME ,
at a URL; see
.Sx MCP .
.It Ic /usage
This session's requests, tokens in and out, what reading from the
provider's prompt cache saved, the cost and the price it was worked out
at, and the context in use against the model's window.
.It Ic /remember Oo # Ns Ar tag , Ns ... Oc Ar fact
Remember
.Ar fact
for every future conversation, not just this one.
.It Ic /forget Ar tag | word
Forget the facts tagged with, or containing,
.Ar word .
.It Ic /my-memory Op Ar tag
List what is remembered.
.It Ic /notes Op Ic forget Ar n | word
List the model's notes on this project, numbered (see
.Sx Project notes ) ,
or forget note
.Ar n ,
or every note containing
.Ar word .
.It Ic /test Op Ar command
Run the project's test command with
.Pa /bin/sh :
.Ar command ,
which becomes the project's from then on, else the one given before,
else
.Cm test_cmd .
A project with a test command offers the model
.Ic check .
Success is only reported; a failure is put to the model, with the end of
its output, as a turn asking what to fix.
.It Ic /stage Op Ic drop
Say whether the session is staged, working on a copy of the project, each
turn's changes coming as one diff to apply, and offer what the copy
holds; with
.Ic drop ,
discard it.
See
.Sx THE STAGE .
.It Ic /safety Op Ic auto | safe
Say which safety the session has, or set it:
.Ic auto
works on a copy, as above;
.Ic safe
asks about each change and has no
.Ic shell .
Remembered for the project; see
.Sx THE STAGE .
.It Ic /quit
Leave; so does
.Ic /exit ,
and C-d on an empty input.
.El
.Sh MODES
A mode changes the instructions the model works under, and in three
what it may do.
S-Tab steps through them, and
.Cm mode
in the configuration chooses the first.
.Bl -tag -width improve
.It Cm chat
Questions, explanations and small edits.
The default: it answers what is asked, and proposes changes only when
asked for one.
.It Cm review
Review what you name, else the uncommitted changes, and report problems
by severity with
.Ar path : Ns Ar line .
Offered no tool that changes files, and no
.Ic shell .
.It Cm improve
Propose a few concrete simplifications, each with a reason.
Offered no tool that changes files, and no
.Ic shell .
.It Cm pair
Build a feature with you: ask when the goal is unclear, plan, then edit
in small steps, stopping after each.
.It Cm build
Lead workers through a larger job.
The model settles the goal with you and puts a plan to you with
.Ic plan ;
until you approve one it edits, moves, removes, delegates and merges nothing,
though it may look around with
.Ic shell .
Then it hands each step to a worker with
.Ic delegate ,
or does it itself when no
.Cm workers
are set, reviews what each did, merges or drops each staged worker's
diff with
.Ic merge ,
runs
.Ic check ,
and fixes only small things itself.
A turn here is bounded by
.Cm build_steps
and
.Cm build_budget
in place of
.Cm max_steps
and
.Cm turn_budget .
It works best staged; set while safe,
.Ic /mode build
says so.
.El
.Pp
.Nm
describes itself to the model, its commands and modes and where the
configuration is, so you can ask it how to do something in
.Nm .
.Sh ATTACHMENTS
Words starting with
.Ql @
in a message attach context.
The transcript shows the message as typed; the model gets the
attachments too.
An
.Ql @
starts a reference only at the start of the text or after a blank or an
opening parenthesis, so an email address is left alone.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It @ Ns Ar path
The file.
.It @ Ns Ar path : Ns Ar from Ns - Ns Ar to , @ Ns Ar path : Ns Ar line
Those lines of it.
.It @ Ns Ar dir Ns /
A listing of the directory, a name a line, a subdirectory with a slash.
.It @ Ns Qq Ar path
The same, for a path with spaces in it; the range, if any, follows the
closing quote.
An unquoted path ends at the next space.
There are no escapes in the quotes.
.It @ Ns Ar image
An image, a
.Pa .png , .jpg , .jpeg , .gif
or
.Pa .webp ,
for a model that can see.
.El
.Pp
A file mentioned twice is attached once.
A file that cannot be read, or holds a NUL byte, is not attached.
Attachments are what you ask for, so they are not subject to
.Sx THE PATH FENCE .
.Sh CONTEXT FILES
At the first turn
.Nm
looks for
.Pa AGENTS.md
and
.Pa CLAUDE.md
in your home directory,
.Pa ~/.claude ,
and every directory from just below home down to the working directory,
so that a file in a parent of several projects counts for each of them.
Outside your home directory it looks in
.Pa ~ ,
.Pa ~/.claude ,
the project root and the working directory.
Last, it looks in its own directory for the project,
.Pa ~/.local/share/arno/projects/ Ns Ar root ,
where what other tools learned of a project is kept outside its
repository;
.Xr arno-claude 1
writes Claude Code's memory and rules there.
It lists what it found and asks once for the project whether to send them,
and keeps your answer in
.Pa context.json
beside the conversations.
If you say yes, they go into every request as standing
instructions, the more general first and the more specific last, where
they read as the last word.
With no answer kept and nobody to ask, as in an editor's
session in the background, they are left out, and arno says so.
Set
.Cm context_files
to skip the question.
Asking is the default because a context file comes with a repository,
not from you, and what it names may be anywhere on the disk.
.Pp
A context file can pull in others by naming them with
.Ql @ ,
as in
.Ql "See @docs/style.md" :
relative to the file that names them, or to your home directory with
.Ql @~/ ,
to five levels.
The list you are asked about includes every import.
.Pp
Context files are not part of the conversation, so
.Ic /clear ,
.Ic /compact
and
.Ic /load
keep them.
.Sh AHEAD OF TIME
Some of what a model would search for,
.Nm
can tell it before it asks, by fixed rules, with no model involved.
Each kind works only inside a project with a version-control directory;
outside one the project is the bare working directory, which may be your
home.
.Ss The project map
At the first turn
.Nm
surveys the project's files and puts a map of about thirty lines into
every request of the session: the number of files at the top level and
in each top-level directory, up to fourteen, each with its three
commonest extensions; the build files and manifests; where the tests
are; and the first paragraph of the top-level README.
It is made once, so a file added during the session is not on it.
Hidden files are not counted.
Set
.Cm project_map
to
.Ql no
to turn it off.
.Ss Prefetch
Before a message goes out,
.Nm
looks for names of code in what you typed: a word in backticks, or one
shaped like code.
It looks up the first five found as
.Ic def
would, and attaches the definition of each one that has exactly one,
three at most, as if you had attached those lines with
.Ql @ .
Each shows in the transcript under your message as the
.Ic def
call it saves the model from making.
Set
.Cm prefetch
to
.Ql no
to turn it off.
.Ss Project notes
What one session finds by searching, the next would otherwise search for
again.
The model keeps such findings with the
.Ic note
tool, a line each: where a feature lives, a layout it did not expect,
how to build.
Every request carries the project's notes, kept by project root, thirty
at most; with thirty, a new note is refused.
A note that names paths, none of which exist any more, is dropped, and
you are told.
.Pp
A note is written without asking, as it changes nothing in the project,
but it shows in the transcript, and
.Ic /notes
lists and forgets them.
When staged, the notes a turn kept come with its diff, as lines starting
.Ql "# note:" ,
since a wrong note misleads every later session and the diff is where
you look.
Look at them after working in a repository you do not trust: a file
there could have talked the model into a note, and a note outlives the
session.
.Sh TOOLS
The model can call these on its own.
Each call shows in the transcript, and TAB opens it.
Calls of
.Ic read , find , grep , def , outline , git , search
and
.Ic fetch
that repeat one already made in the same turn, arguments and all, are
not run: the model is told it already has the answer.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic read
A file, each line numbered, in part if asked: up to 2000 lines from a
line.
.It Ic find
Files by glob, matched against the file's name and each directory's on
its path, or the whole path when the glob holds a
.Ql / .
Hidden names, and what
.Pa .gitignore
and
.Pa .ignore
files exclude, are left out; so are binary files.
.It Ic grep
A fixed text, not a pattern, in one file or a tree, choosing files as
.Ic find
does.
With
.Ar context ,
that many lines either side of each match, up to 10.
The result is cut at about 20000 bytes, ending with how many matches
were left out.
.It Ic def
Where a name is defined, and the whole definition with the comment above
it, numbered as
.Ic read
numbers lines; at most ten, over a file or a tree.
.It Ic outline
The declarations of a file or a tree, a numbered line each; in Markdown,
the headings.
.Pp
Both know declarations by their shape, with no parser: a line that
starts, past modifiers such as
.Li export
or
.Li static ,
with a declaring word
.Pq Li function , proc , procedure , class , def , fn , type , No ... ,
a signature followed by its body, or
.Li const ,
.Li let
or
.Li var
holding an arrow function or a function expression.
A definition ends where the indentation comes back to its own.
A shape outside these goes unseen, and the model is told to grep
instead.
.It Ic git
Read-only
.Cm status , diff , log , show , blame ,
.Cm branch
(listing only),
.Cm merge-base , ls-tree
and
.Cm rev-list ,
run without a shell.
Options that would write files, run external diff or textconv
programs, or compare files outside the repository are refused.
.It Ic edit
An exact, unique replacement, or a new file.
Its diff is shown and needs your yes.
If
.Cm lsp. Ns Ar ext
names a language server for the file's type, the result ends with what
the server finds wrong with the file: its errors and warnings, at most
ten, each as
.Ql path:line:col: severity: message ,
or
.Ql no errors .
.It Ic move , remove
Rename or delete one file, with your yes.
Never a directory, and never onto a file that exists, and never a file an
editor has open (see
.Sx EDITORS ) .
.It Ic check
The project's test command, as set by
.Ic /test
or
.Cm test_cmd ;
the model gets the verdict and the end of the output.
It may name one of the checks of
.Cm check.NAME
instead, and give files for its
.Ql {} :
each must exist inside the project and may not start with
.Ql - ,
and is quoted for the shell.
A check whose command has no
.Ql {}
takes no files; one that has it, given none, runs with it removed, the whole
suite.
The model chooses which check and on what files, never the command, so
it runs only what you typed yourself, and without asking.
While staged it runs in the jail, as
.Ic shell
does: the model may have edited the Makefile it runs.
Offered only when there is a command or a named check; the names and their
commands are in its description.
See
.Sx CAVEATS .
.It Ic run
One of the programs
.Cm run
names; see
.Sx RUN AND TRUST .
.It Ic shell
A command line, run in the jail, in the project; see
.Sx THE JAIL .
It writes in the stage's copy.
Offered only staged (safety
.Ic auto )
where the jail can run.
.It Ic skill
One of your skills, by name: the text of
.Pa ~/.config/arno/skills/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .md
but its first line, which says what kind of work it is for.
The skills are listed in the tool's description, each with that line,
so the model reads one when the work calls for it, and a long one costs
nothing until then.
They are yours, kept for every project: how you write a review's
comments, a ticket, a commit.
A name holds only letters, digits and
.Ql -_. ;
another file is passed over.
Offered only when there is one.
.It Ic note
A line kept for later sessions on this project; see
.Sx Project notes .
Offered only inside a project.
.It Ic search
The web, through a SearXNG instance, as titles, URLs and snippets only;
the model is told to read two or three of the pages with
.Ic fetch .
Offered only when
.Cm searxng
is set.
.It Ic fetch
A web page, by its URL, as text: its title, then its text as a text
browser shows it, scripts and styles left out; a plain-text, JSON or
Markdown body as it is.
At most 20000 bytes come at once; a longer page ends with a line saying
how much is left and the
.Ar offset
to fetch again with.
.Li http:
and
.Li https:
only.
.It Ic editor , open
What you have open in your text editor, and a file shown there at a line;
see
.Sx EDITORS .
Offered only while an editor is reachable.
.It Ic delegate
One self-contained step of a larger job, given to a worker: a
conversation of its own on the model the step's
.Cm model
names, one of
.Cm workers ,
or the first of them,
with the brief, the reports of earlier workers of the turn (the
newest four) and the tools of the session less
.Ic delegate
and
.Ic merge .
Only its report goes back to the model, with the steps it took and
what it cost.
A step may name a
.Cm role ,
one of those kept in
.Nm Ns 's
own directory for the project,
.Pa ~/.local/share/arno/projects/ Ns Ar root Ns Pa /roles/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .md ,
whose first line says what it is for and the rest is given to the
worker with the brief; they are listed in the tool's description.
.Xr arno-claude 1
writes Claude Code's agents there.
A worker is paid out of the turn's budget: the step's
.Cm budget
if it names one, at most what the turn has left, and all of that if
not; a turn with nothing left delegates nothing.
Its budget, not
.Cm max_steps ,
is what bounds it, as a cheaper model takes more steps; it stops after
.Cm build_steps
all the same.
Staged, each worker is numbered and works in a copy of its own, made
from the stage's copy as it stands, beside it in the cache: its edits
are not asked about, and its
.Ic shell ,
.Ic check
and
.Ic run
are jailed there.
When it ends, even at its step cap, what it changed goes back as one
diff, kept in the scratch directory as
.Pa worker-N.diff
and summed up file by file in the result, and its copy is removed.
Safe, there is no copy to make one from: a worker works in the project
itself, each change asked, as the model's own.
The description lists the models with their list prices per million
tokens, for the model to weigh each step: a stronger one for a hard
step, a cheaper one for mechanical work.
A model not listed is refused.
The report names the model.
Offered only when
.Cm workers
is set.
.It Ic merge
A staged worker's diff, by its number, applied to the stage's copy or
dropped, and then removed.
It applies whole or not at all: one that no longer fits the copy
changes nothing, and is kept, to be dropped.
Offered only with
.Ic delegate ,
while staged.
.It Ic plan
The plan of
.Cm build
mode, shown to you to approve or decline with a reason, and kept as
.Pa plan.md
in the conversation's scratch directory.
The approval is the conversation's: the last answer to
.Ic plan
in it counts, so
.Ic /new
starts without one.
With nobody to ask, the answer is no.
The plan stands apart from its call, as a reply does.
In the terminal,
.Ic e
opens it in
.Ev VISUAL
or
.Ev EDITOR ,
as C-x C-e does the input, and it is asked again as you left it; an
edited plan you approve goes back to the model whole, to carry out in
place of its own.
Offered only in
.Cm build
mode.
.It MCP tools
Whatever the configured MCP servers offer, each named
.Ar server Ns _ Ns Ar tool ;
see
.Sx MCP .
.It A host's tools
The ones a program that runs
.Nm Fl \-serve
gave; see
.Sx SERVE .
.El
.Pp
In
.Cm review
and
.Cm improve
modes,
.Ic edit , move , remove
and
.Ic shell
are not offered.
.Pp
After a no to any of these questions,
.Nm
asks
.Ql "why not?" ;
what you type goes to the model with the refusal.
A change you declined, proposed again unchanged, is not put to you
again: the model is told your first answer instead.
.Ic /clear , /new
and
.Ic /load
forget what was declined.
.Pp
A turn takes at most 30 steps of tool calls, or
.Cm max_steps .
Then the model is asked, with no more tools to run, to say what is done,
what it found and what it will do next, so that a long turn ends in
something you can read.
Four steps in a row in which every call failed end the turn the same
way, sooner; so does a turn that has spent
.Cm turn_budget .
.Pp
A result is cut at 32000 bytes.
Old long results are cut from the history, in the saved conversation
too, once it passes
.Cm prune
bytes.
A worker's report can't be had again by calling again, so each is kept
whole in the scratch directory, as
.Pa report- Ns Ar n Ns Pa .md ,
named on its first line, and a cut one says to read it there.
.Sh THE PATH FENCE
The file tools
.Po
.Ic read , find , grep , def , outline , edit , move , remove ,
and paths given to
.Ic git
or
.Ic run
.Pc
reach, without asking, only the project: the nearest directory, at most
two levels above the working directory, that holds
.Pa .git ,
.Pa .sauce ,
.Pa .hg ,
.Pa .jj
or
.Pa .svn ,
or else the working directory itself, with everything below it.
Paths are compared after every symbolic link is followed, so neither
.Pa ../..
nor a link pointing out of the project gets past.
.Pp
For anything else you are asked, with three answers:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It y
This once.
.It n
No; also what RET, Esc and C-c mean.
.It a
Always: that path and everything below it, or for a file, the directory
that holds it (never
.Pa /
or your home directory, where it stays the file), until
.Ic /clear ,
.Ic /new
or
.Ic /load .
.El
.Pp
The
.Cm grant
key is accepted but not read in this
.Nm ;
answer always instead.
.Ss The scratch directory
Each conversation has a directory of the model's own,
.Pa ~/.cache/arno/scratch/ Ns Ar id ,
for fixture trees, throwaway files and output to compare, which would
otherwise go into the project or ask to leave it.
The system prompt names it.
It is inside the fence, and more:
.Ic edit ,
.Ic move
and
.Ic remove
there are not asked about, since nothing of yours is there.
A path that resolves out of it, through a link the model's code left
there, is judged as any other.
.Ic check , run
and
.Ic shell
get its path as
.Ev ARNO_SCRATCH .
.Pp
It lasts as long as its conversation:
.Ic /load
brings it back,
.Ic /compact
takes it along,
.Ic /clear
empties it and
.Ic /new
starts another.
At start-up those whose conversation is no longer saved, and that have
not changed in a day, are removed.
.Sh THE STAGE
By default
.Nm
is staged (safety
.Ic auto ) :
it moves into a copy of the project, a
.Xr git-worktree 1
in
.Pa ~/.cache/arno/stage/ Ns Ar pid ,
and makes it the zone.
There, as in the scratch directory, the model's edits are not asked
about one by one,
.Ic check
builds and tests the copy, and your tree is not touched.
.Ic shell
is offered there.
When a turn ends with anything changed, you are asked once, with the
whole diff: yes applies it to your tree, no keeps it in the copy, to be
offered again, with whatever comes next, after the following turn.
The diff is also written to the scratch directory as
.Pa stage.diff ,
for a run with no one to answer; it is removed once applied or dropped.
.Pp
Safety
.Ic safe
is not staged: each edit, move and remove is asked, and
.Ic shell
is not offered.
A session starts safe when the configuration's
.Cm safety
or the project's
.Pa stage.json
says so, or when no stage can be made, in which case it says why at
start-up:
.Ql "safety: safe, each change asked, since there is no stage: WHY" .
.Pp
The copy starts from your tree as it is, not from the last commit: your
uncommitted changes and new files come along, and the diff holds only
the model's work.
Ignored files, such as build output, stay behind, but for the
dependencies:
.Pa node_modules , .venv
and
.Pa venv
at the top of your tree come into the copy as links to yours, left out
of the diff.
In the jail they are read-only, so an install fails there; the model is
told to change the manifest and ask you to install.
Applying is
.Xr git-apply 1 ,
all or nothing: if you changed one of the same lines meanwhile, nothing
is applied and the work stays in the copy.
git's hooks are not run, nothing is committed, and the copy is removed
when
.Nm
leaves.
It needs a git repository with at least one commit.
A project under other version control, or with no commit yet, has no
stage, and the session is safe.
.Pp
The copy's own
.Pa .git
file, which points at its git directory, is the model's to write only
with approval, since git reads settings from it that run programs.
.Pp
The pending work belongs to the conversation:
.Fl \-resume
or
.Ic /load ,
staged, bring it back into the new copy from
.Pa stage.diff ,
to be offered again after the next turn, unless that copy already holds
changes of its own.
.Pp
.Ic /stage
with no argument says whether the session is staged and offers what the
copy holds.
.Ic /stage drop
discards that, putting the copy back as it was when last applied.
.Ic /safety
with no argument says which safety the session has.
.Ic /safety safe
leaves the copy, and is refused while it holds changes:
.Ql "the copy holds changes: /stage to apply them, /stage drop to discard them" .
.Ic /safety auto
and
.Ic /safety safe
are remembered for the project, in
.Pa stage.json :
its next session starts the same way, whatever
.Cm safety
says.
.Pp
A copy whose
.Nm
was killed by a signal or crashed is removed as the next one is made.
.Sh THE JAIL
.Ic shell
runs a command line with
.Pa /bin/sh
.Fl c
in the stage's copy, inside a sandbox made of Linux's unprivileged
namespaces, with no root and no helper program:
.Bl -tag -width "mount" -compact
.It user
The same uid and gid as outside, with no power over what it does not own
and no way to gain any.
.It mount
The whole file tree read-only, except the copy, the scratch directory,
the directories
.Cm jail_rw
names, and a fresh
.Pa /tmp ,
.Pa /dev/shm
and
.Pa /run .
The places a login keeps its keys look empty.
.It net
No network at all, not even the loopback interface; with
.Cm jail_net No = Cm yes ,
the network as outside, loopback included.
.It pid
The command sees only its own processes; when its first one ends, or
the time runs out, all are killed.
.It ipc
No shared memory or queues shared with the rest.
.El
.Pp
It gets no input, no terminal and no display, and its environment is cut
to
.Ev PATH , HOME , USER , LOGNAME , LANG ,
every
.Ev LC_*
and
.Ev TZ ,
with
.Ev TERM Ns = Ns Li dumb ,
.Ev TMPDIR Ns = Ns Li /tmp
and
.Ev ARNO_SCRATCH .
Your environment holds your keys, and none of them reach it.
.Pp
It runs without asking, since the sandbox is the fence.
Its arguments are
.Ar command
and
.Ar timeout ,
in seconds: 120 by default, 600 at most.
The model gets
.Ql "exit N: COMMAND" ,
.Ql "timed out after N s: COMMAND"
or
.Ql "killed: more than 4 MB of output; the last of it:" ,
then the end of the output.
A command that runs past its timeout is killed, as is one that prints
more than 4 MB; ^C stops one that runs.
A sandbox that cannot be made is an error, never a command run outside
it.
.Pp
.Ic shell
is offered only while staged, where the jail can run, unless
.Ql "shell = no" .
It writes in the copy, never in your own tree, where it could rewrite
.Pa .git
or the Makefile, which git and
.Ic check
then run outside the sandbox, with no diff shown.
It is not offered when safe: there every read outside the project goes
through the path fence's question, which a jailed shell, reading whatever
you can, would pass by.
It is not offered in
.Cm review
or
.Cm improve
mode either.
A call of it when it is not on offer is refused:
.Ql "error: shell is not on offer" .
.Pp
The jail needs Linux 5.12 or later, with unprivileged user namespaces
enabled, on x86_64; where it cannot run, the tool is not offered.
.Sh RUN AND TRUST
.Ic run
starts one of the programs the
.Cm run
key names, and
.Cm ls wc head file
and
.Cm stat
always, with the model's arguments, and gives back its exit status
and output.
There is no shell: the arguments go to the program as they are, so
there is nothing to quote and no pipe or redirection.
The program gets no input, no terminal and no display
.Pq Ev DISPLAY , WAYLAND_DISPLAY No are unset ,
so one that wants a person fails at once instead of waiting; ^C stops
one that runs on.
It sees
.Ev ARNO_SCRATCH .
While staged it runs in the jail, with the copy and
.Ev ARNO_SCRATCH
its only writable places and no network, but as
.Cm jail_rw
and
.Cm jail_net
say; safe, it is not jailed.
The tool's description names the programs allowed and those that run
without asking, so a model need not guess.
.Pp
Each call shows the command and asks.
.Ql a
runs it and allows that program without asking until
.Ic /clear , /new
or
.Ic /load .
Programs named by
.Cm trust ,
and
.Cm ls wc head file
and
.Cm stat ,
which only read and are always allowed as if in
.Cm run_ro ,
start without asking, as if answered always; their arguments are still
held to the fence.
Several commands may go in one call, parted by a lone
.Ql \&; :
one question names the programs not yet trusted, and the commands run
one after another, each result under its command line.
Environment variables go in
.Ql env ,
but only the names
.Cm run_env
lists.
.Pp
The program is your choice; its options are the model's.
List only programs that cannot be talked into running others:
never a shell, an interpreter,
.Xr env 1
or
.Xr xargs 1 .
Three kinds of argument are refused whatever the program:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Any option to
.Xr man 1 :
it takes a section and a page, and nothing else.
.Ql "man --html=PROGRAM"
runs any browser it is named, even with no terminal, and its pager
option runs a pager of the caller's naming, so no option can be allowed.
.It
.Fl x ,
.Fl X ,
.Fl \-exec
and
.Fl \-exec\-batch
to
.Xr fd 1 ,
which run any program for each file found.
.It
A path glued to an option without an
.Ql = ,
as in
.Fl o Ns Pa ../x.png :
the fence judges words, and would not see it.
.El
.Pp
Every other argument is checked against
.Sx THE PATH FENCE ,
an option's after its
.Ql = .
For the programs
.Cm run_ro
lists, those that only read, only the arguments that are files or
directories are held to the fence, as for
.Ic git ,
so a pattern such as
.Ql /src$
asks nothing.
.Sh MCP
MCP, the Model Context Protocol, lets
.Nm
offer a model tools it does not carry.
A server is any program that speaks it over standard input and output,
or a URL that speaks it over HTTP.
Each
.Cm mcp
line of the configuration,
.Ql "mcp = NAME COMMAND ARG ..."
or
.Ql "mcp = NAME URL" ,
names one; the
.Ql =
is required, and a URL starts with
.Ql http://
or
.Ql https:// .
.Nm
starts each command as the session begins, shakes hands, asks for its
tools, and offers each as
.Ar NAME Ns _ Ns Ar TOOL ,
in every mode.
A byte a provider would not take in a name becomes
.Ql _ ,
and a name is cut at 64 bytes.
.Pp
Every call is asked about, with the arguments shown (cut at 300 bytes),
and runs only on a yes, unless
.Cm trust
names the tool, its server, or a prefix of its name ending in
.Ql * ;
the answer
.Ql a
trusts the tool until the conversation changes, as for
.Ic run .
MCP tools are not fenced and not subject to a
host's list of builtins.
A server runs in a process group of its own, so that ^C stops the turn and
not the server; its standard error is thrown away.
A server that does not answer when it starts is given up on.
What goes wrong with one is shown, and the rest are attached.
At the end every server is closed and any left is killed.
.Pp
A server at a URL is sent each message as a POST, and answers with JSON
or with a stream of events; the session it gives at the start goes with
every message after, and is ended at the end.
A session the server has forgotten is begun again, once.
If the server needs a login,
.Cm mcp_token. Ns Ar NAME
gives a command, run once as the server is attached, whose first line
of output is sent as
.Ql "Authorization: Bearer" ;
so a secret stays in a password store:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mcp              = linear https://mcp.linear.app/mcp
mcp_token.linear = pass linear/mcp
.Ed
.Pp
A server that answers 401 is not attached, and says it needs a login.
.Pp
.Ic /mcp login Ar NAME
logs in by OAuth instead, through the browser: the server's
authorization server is found and
.Nm
registered there, and the page to log in at is shown, and opened by
.Xr xdg-open 1
if there is one; a human on another machine opens it there.
The browser is waited for five minutes at most, or until ^C.
The login is kept, and the server attached with it.
A session after has its access token again from the login kept;
a token is renewed a minute before it runs out, and once if the
server refuses it.
A login that has run out is said, with the command that makes a new
one.
.Cm mcp_token. Ns Ar NAME
goes before a login kept, and a login kept for another URL than the
configuration's is passed by.
.Pp
A result's content blocks are joined: a text block as it is, any other
as its type in brackets.
A server's own error makes the result start with
.Ql error .
Not followed: pagination of the tool list, resources, prompts, a server's
requests of
.Nm ,
and servers over HTTP.
.Sh SERVE
With
.Fl \-serve ,
.Nm
runs one session for the program that started it, a word processor say,
which shows the conversation in its own way and may give the model tools
of its own.
The two talk over
.Nm Ns 's
standard input and output in messages, each a netstring holding a run of
netstrings: the program sends
.Cm hello
first, with its name, the project root and its tools, then lines of
input, cancels, answers to questions and the results of its tools;
.Nm
sends what the terminal would have shown, and asks the program
everything it would have asked you: an edit, a path outside the project, a
program to run, a call of an MCP tool, the stage's diff.
A refusal may come with its reason, which the model reads as it would
yours.
The whole protocol is in
.Xr arno-serve 5 .
.Pp
Nothing else is written to standard output, which becomes a copy of
standard error, so that nothing a tool or a library prints can land in
the middle of a message.
The session works in the root
.Cm hello
names, and fences tools to the project found from there.
.Cm hello
may withhold builtin tools, which the model then neither sees nor can
call.
The session ends when the program says
.Cm bye
or closes the pipe, with status 0, and its stage's copy is removed.
.Sh EDITORS
A text editor that speaks
.Xr arno-peer 5 ,
.Xr dwa 1
for one, and
.Nm
can talk to each other.
.Pp
The editor asks
.Nm :
in dwa,
.Ic :ask
sends the lines of a range, the cursor's line by default, and a question,
which
.Nm
takes as a turn, and answers where you asked, in the editor, when it ends.
.Ic :ask!
asks in a new conversation.
With no question, the lines go into
.Nm Ns 's
input as an
.Li @
reference, for you to finish.
A question in a turn waits for it to end.
The ask shows in the transcript as coming from the editor, with its file and
lines, and not as something you typed; the model is told so, and that the
lines are text, not instructions.
Every change it leads to is asked about here, as always.
Only a terminal's
.Nm
takes an ask.
One run with
.Fl \-serve
belongs to its host, and says so to the editor.
.Pp
.Nm
asks the editor that asked last, else the socket in
.Ev ARNO_EDITOR ,
else the best
.Pa dwa- Ns Ar pid Ns Pa .sock
in
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR :
one whose process works in
.Nm Ns 's
directory, before the newest.
While one is reachable, checked at the start of each turn, and not while the
session is staged:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
the model is offered
.Ic editor ,
what you have open and where, and
.Ic open ,
which shows a file at a line;
.It
.Ic read
gives a file as the editor has it, unsaved changes included, and so does the
text an
.Ic edit
is made on;
.It
an
.Ic edit ,
once you approve it, is made by the editor, in front of you, as one of your own
undo steps, and the file saved if it had no other unsaved changes;
.It
.Ic move
and
.Ic remove
refuse a file the editor has open.
.El
.Pp
Anything the editor lacks,
.Nm
does on the disk.
The sockets are private to your user; see
.Xr arno-peer 5
for them, and for how a reply socket that an ask names is checked.
.Sh ATTACH
Every
.Nm
on a terminal, and every one run with
.Fl \-serve ,
is a session others can watch.
.Nm
.Fl \-attach
shows one in this terminal: the transcript so far, then everything as it
happens, on the same screen with copy mode and all.
What you type goes to the session as if typed there, and C-c stops its
turn.
Questions stay with the session's owner, its terminal or the program
driving it; while one waits, the status line here says so, and the answer
shows in the tool call's result.
.Pp
With a
.Ar pid ,
that session is shown; without one, a session working in this directory,
else the newest.
.Fl l
lists the sessions instead.
.Ic /quit ,
.Ic /exit
and C-d leave the session running.
.Pp
A viewer that falls more than a megabyte behind is dropped, so a stuck
terminal never holds up the session.
The socket is
.Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/arno/ Ns Ar pid Ns .view ,
or, with no such directory,
.Pa run/
in
.Nm Ns 's
cache; its directory is mode 700, and a connection from another user is
dropped.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Pa ~/.config/arno/config
holds
.Ar key No = Ar value
lines; blank lines, lines starting with
.Ql #
and lines with no
.Ql =
are ignored.
Blanks around either part are dropped.
A line that begins with a blank or a tab goes on with the setting above
it: its text, trimmed, is added to the value after one blank, so
.Bd -literal -offset indent
trust = man fd
    linear-server_list_issues
    linear-server_get_user
.Ed
.Pp
is
.Ql "trust = man fd linear-server_list_issues linear-server_get_user" .
A
.Ql #
line inside does not end the setting; an empty line, a section header or
another setting does.
An indented line with nothing to go on with is warned of and passed by.
A key given twice is the last one's, except
.Cm mcp ,
which is given once for each server.
Put
.Cm provider
first, since it resets the other provider settings.
What is wrong with the file, an unknown provider, a number that is not
one, or a section that names no directory or sets a key it may not, is
shown as a warning at start-up, a line each, and the rest is read as if
the line were not there.
Sections, below, give a tree of projects settings of its own.
.Bl -tag -width "context_window"
.It Cm provider
One of the names under
.Sx PROVIDERS .
.It Cm model
The model's name, as the provider knows it.
.It Cm url
The API's base URL, to override the provider's.
.It Cm key
The name of the environment variable holding the API key, not the key
itself.
.It Cm dialect
.Cm openai
or
.Cm anthropic :
the wire format of the API at
.Cm url .
.It Cm timeout
Seconds a request may take in all; 0 for no limit.
.It Cm max_retries
Times a request whose connection failed is made again; 0 by default.
.It Cm retry_backoff
Seconds before the first retry, doubled each time; 2 by default.
.It Cm effort
Reasoning effort per model, as
.Ar model : Ns Ar level
pairs separated by blanks, such as
.Ql "kimi-k3:high deepseek-v4-pro:high" ;
sent in place of
.Cm reasoning_effort
when that model is the one in use.
.It Cm mode
The mode a session starts in.
.It Cm workers
The models the lead may hand a step to, blank-separated, on the same
provider; a step names one, or runs on the first.
List them cheapest first, so that a step names a dearer one only when
it needs it.
Without
.Cm workers
.Ic delegate
is not offered.
The older
.Cm worker ,
one model, the one a step runs on when it names none, is still read.
Each model has its own
.Cm effort
entry, if any.
.It Cm max_steps
Steps of tool calls a turn takes before it hands back; 30 by default.
.It Cm prune
Bytes of history past which old tool results are cut, 200000 by default;
0 never cuts.
.It Cm turn_budget
Dollars at list price one turn may spend, workers included; past it the
model is asked to say where it got to and stop.
Unset, or 0, is no limit;
.Ic /budget
changes it for a session.
.It Cm build_steps
Steps of tool calls a turn takes in
.Cm build
mode, in place of
.Cm max_steps ;
200 by default.
.It Cm build_budget
Dollars at list price a turn may spend in
.Cm build
mode, in place of
.Cm turn_budget ;
3.00 by default, 0 for no limit.
.It Cm searxng
The base URL of a SearXNG instance for
.Ic search .
SearXNG disables JSON output by default; add
.Ql json
under
.Cm search: formats:
in its
.Pa settings.yml .
.It Cm test_cmd
What
.Ic /test
and
.Ic check
run in a project
.Ic /test
has not been given a command for.
.It Cm check.NAME
A named check, for any NAME of letters, digits,
.Ql -
and
.Ql _ :
.Ql check.unit = npx vitest run {} .
The
.Ic check
tool may run it by name, with files for the
.Ql {}
in the command.
Given more than once, as a key of a project's section or not, a deeper
section's wins.
.It Cm safety
.Ql auto
(the default) starts a session staged;
.Ql safe
starts it asking each change, in every project that
.Ic /safety
has not been given in.
.It Cm shell
.Ql no
withdraws the
.Ic shell
tool; it is offered while staged, where the jail can run.
.It Cm jail_net
.Ql yes
leaves the network on in the jail of
.Ic shell ,
.Ic check
and
.Ic run ,
for tests that fetch; off by default.
.It Cm jail_rw
Directories, separated by blanks, that the jail leaves writable beside
the copy and the scratch directory, for tests that keep a cache in the
home, such as
.Pa ~/.npm .
Each is absolute or starts with
.Ql ~ ;
one that is not a directory when a command runs is passed over.
The jail takes at most six.
.It Cm lsp. Ns Ar ext
The language server, a command and its arguments, for the files whose
name ends in
.Pf . Ar ext ,
as
.Ql lsp.ts = typescript-language-server --stdio ,
.Ql lsp.raz = raz-lsp
or
.Ql lsp.cn = concha-lsp .
It is started on the first edit of such a file in a turn, rooted at
the project (the copy while staged), and stopped when the turn ends.
After each edit it is told the file's whole new text, and its
diagnostics end the edit's result; it is waited for up to 10 seconds
to start and 2 seconds for each edit's diagnostics.
While staged it runs in the jail, with no network and nothing
writable but the jail's own
.Pa /tmp ,
where
.Ev XDG_STATE_HOME
and
.Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME
point.
One that does not start, or breaks, is said once and not started again
in the session.
Its standard error is thrown away.
.It Cm run
Program names, separated by blanks, that
.Ic run
may start, besides
.Cm ls wc head file
and
.Cm stat ,
which it may always start; see
.Sx RUN AND TRUST .
.It Cm run_ro
Of those, the ones that only read; see
.Sx RUN AND TRUST .
.It Cm run_env
Environment variable names that
.Ic run
may set for its programs; none by default.
.It Cm trust
Programs that start without asking, MCP tools that are called without
asking, and MCP servers all of whose tools are.
A word is a program
.Cm run
names; or an MCP tool's full name, as offered
.Pq Ql linear-server_list_issues ;
or an MCP server's name in
.Cm mcp
.Pq Ql linear-server ;
or a tool name's start followed by
.Ql * ,
as
.Ql linear-server_get_* ,
which also trusts the tools the server adds later under that prefix.
A bare
.Ql *
trusts nothing, and a
.Ql *
means nothing in a program's name.
Example:
.Pp
.Dl trust = man fd linear-server_list_* linear
.Pp
See
.Sx MCP .
.It Cm context_files
.Cm ask ,
the default,
.Cm yes
or
.Cm no :
whether to ask before sending the context files (once for the project;
the answer is kept), send them without asking, or never look for them.
.Cm yes
and
.Cm no
override a kept answer.
.It Cm project_map , prefetch
.Cm no
turns off the map or the prefetch; both are on by default.
.It Cm mcp
A name, then the command that runs an MCP server, or its URL; one line
for each.
.It Cm mcp_token. Ns Ar NAME
A command whose first line of output is the bearer token for the MCP
server
.Ar NAME
at a URL; see
.Sx MCP .
.It Cm price
.Ar in out Op Ar cached :
dollars per million tokens of input, output and input read from the
provider's cache, which defaults to a tenth of
.Ar in .
Overrides the built-in price of the configured model.
.It Cm context_window
The model's context window in tokens, for the status line.
.El
.Pp
The keys
.Cm keys , cache_tools , cache_ttl , find_cmd , grep_cmd
and
.Cm grant
are the Pascal arno's.
They are accepted, so that its file reads, and ignored.
.Pp
Of the other keys, only these are sent, in the provider's request:
.Cm temperature , top_p
and
.Cm max_tokens
(Claude needs the last, and has 8192 when it is not set), and the header
.Cm anthropic_version
of a Claude provider.
Any other key is read and then dropped, without a word.
.Pp
A reasoning model's thinking is not shown, but it is kept for the rest of
the turn and sent back with each tool result, since some models lose the
thread without it.
It is dropped once the turn ends and never saved.
.Ss Sections
A line
.No \&[ Ns Ar dir Ns \&]
begins a section: the settings after it, up to the next section, are for
the project whose root is
.Ar dir
or below it.
.Ar dir
is absolute, or starts with
.Ql ~
for the home; links in it are followed, as in the root.
The settings before the first section are for every project.
At start-up, once the project's root is found, each section that holds it
is applied over them, the shallower first, so the deepest wins; a key's
value is replaced whole, never merged with another's.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
provider = ollama-cloud
run = tiza man fd

[~/r]
run = raz robbie concha sauce make
trust = make
test_cmd = make check

[~/w/webapp]
safety = safe
jail_net = yes
jail_rw = ~/.npm ~/.cache/ms-playwright
.Ed
.Pp
A section may set only what is one project's:
.Cm run , run_ro , run_env , trust , test_cmd , check.NAME , safety , shell ,
.Cm jail_net , jail_rw , context_files , project_map , prefetch ,
.Cm mode , worker , workers , max_steps , turn_budget , build_steps ,
.Cm build_budget
and
.Cm lsp. Ns Ar ext .
The provider's keys, and
.Cm mcp , mcp_token. Ns Ar NAME
and the rest, are for every project, and a section that sets one is
warned of.
A section that names no directory is warned of too, and its settings are
passed over.
.Pp
Only this file holds sections: nothing in a project's tree is read for
them, so a repository cloned grants itself nothing.
What
.Ic /test
and
.Ic /safety
keep for a project
.Pq Pa tests.json , stage.json
is the human's later word, and still outranks a section, as it does the
global settings; the answer about context files
.Pq Pa context.json
counts where
.Cm context_files
is
.Cm ask ,
as before.
.Sh PROVIDERS
.Bl -column "ollama-cloud" "anthropic" "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY" "claude-sonnet-5-5"
.It Em name Ta Em dialect Ta Em key variable Ta Em model
.It Cm ollama Ta openai Ta none Ta deepseek-v4.1-flash:cloud
.It Cm ollama-cloud Ta openai Ta Ev OLLAMA_API_KEY Ta deepseek-v4.1-flash
.It Cm openai Ta openai Ta Ev OPENAI_API_KEY Ta gpt-5
.It Cm gemini Ta openai Ta Ev GEMINI_API_KEY Ta gemini-3.8-flash
.It Cm claude Ta anthropic Ta Ev ANTHROPIC_API_KEY Ta claude-sonnet-5-5
.El
.Pp
The default is
.Cm ollama ,
whose local daemon forwards a
.Ql :cloud
model to Ollama Cloud once
.Ql "ollama signin"
has been run.
The last column is each preset's default model.
.Pp
Claude caches a prompt only where asked, so
.Nm
marks the end of the system prompt and the newest message; each step of a
turn then reads the conversation so far from the cache.
The other providers cache on their own.
.Sh USAGE AND COST
The status line shows how full the context is and what the session has
used:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
CHAT  \[u25CF] auto   claude-sonnet-5-5 @ claude   ctx \[u25B1]\[u25B1]\[u25B1]\[u25B1]\[u25B1]\[u25B1]\[u25B1]\[u25B1] 2%   in 120k out 4.2k \(pc $0.121
.Ed
.Pp
and, as text, to
.Fl \-serve Ns 's
host:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
arno \(pc chat (auto) \(pc claude-sonnet-5-5 @ claude \(pc ctx 23.4k/1.0M 2% \(pc in 120k out 4.2k \(pc $0.121
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql ctx
is what the conversation filled at its last request, out of the model's
window where that is known; until the next request after
.Ic /new , /clear , /load
or
.Ic /compact
it is a guess, marked
.Ql ~ .
Past 60% of the window a
.Ql /compact
reminder joins it.
.Ql in
and
.Ql out
add up every request this run has made, tool steps included: each step
resends the whole conversation, so
.Ql in
grows much faster than
.Ql ctx .
.Pp
The cost is at list price, from a table of the models
.Nm
knows, which is kept by hand and falls behind; the provider's own bill has
the last word.
A
.Ql +
means some requests went to a model with no known price and are left out.
A model run locally by Ollama is free, so only tokens are shown.
Off-peak discounts are not applied.
For a model the table lacks, or a price that has moved, set
.Cm price
and
.Cm context_window .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 for a reply or a session that ended, 1 for a failed request or a
conversation not found, 2 for a mistake on the command line, and 130 for a
turn stopped by ^C.
On the screen, and with
.Fl \-serve ,
it exits 0 when it ends in the ordinary way.
.Pp
.Fl \-attach
exits 1 when there is no session to attach to or no terminal to use, and 2
when its argument is not a process id or
.Fl l .
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the configuration lives, instead of
.Pa ~/.config .
.It Ev XDG_DATA_HOME
Where conversations and memory live, instead of
.Pa ~/.local/share .
.It Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME
Where scratch directories and the stage live, instead of
.Pa ~/.cache .
.It Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the session sockets and pasted images are made.
.It Ev VISUAL , EDITOR
The editor C-x C-e runs, the first one set; else
.Xr vi 1 .
.It Ev ARNO_EDITOR
The socket of the editor to call when none has asked, a path; see
.Sx EDITORS .
.It Ev ARNO_SCRATCH
Set by
.Nm
for
.Ic run , check
and
.Ic shell ,
to the conversation's scratch directory.
.It Ev OLLAMA_API_KEY , OPENAI_API_KEY , GEMINI_API_KEY , ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
API keys, as named by the provider or by
.Cm key .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa ~/.config/arno/config
The configuration, with a section for each tree of projects that has
settings of its own; no file in a project is read for them.
.It Pa ~/.config/arno/skills/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .md
A skill: its first line what kind of work it is for, the rest how you
want it done; see
.Ic skill .
.It Pa ~/.local/share/arno/history
Input history, one entry a line, newest last.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/arno/ Ns Ar yyyymmdd-hhmmss Ns .json
One conversation each, as the Pascal arno wrote it.
Beside the messages,
.Ql turns
keeps a record of each turn.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/arno/memory.json
Remembered facts.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/arno/projects/ Ns Ar root Ns Pa /AGENTS.md
The project's context file kept outside its repository, read last.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/arno/mcp/ Ns Ar NAME Ns .json
The login to the MCP server
.Ar NAME :
its URL, the client id, the token endpoint and the refresh token,
readable by you alone.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/arno/notes.json
Each project's notes, by project root.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/arno/tests.json
Each project's test command, by project root.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/arno/stage.json
Each project's safety, as
.Ic /safety auto
or
.Ic /safety safe
last left it, by project root.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/arno/lock
Locked while one session changes the files above, so that two sessions at
once keep each other's changes.
Each of those files is written whole and renamed into place.
.It Pa ~/.cache/arno/scratch/ Ns Ar id
Each conversation's scratch directory, and in it
.Pa stage.diff ,
what the stage last offered.
.It Pa ~/.cache/arno/stage/ Ns Ar pid
The stage's copy of the project, while a session is staged.
.It Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/arno/ Ns Ar pid Ns .view
The session's socket, which
.Fl \-attach
talks to.
.It Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/arno- Ns Ar pid Ns Pa .sock
The socket editors ask
.Nm
at, in a terminal's session and under
.Fl \-serve ,
mode 700 and removed at the end; none without
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR .
See
.Xr arno-peer 5 .
.It Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/dwa- Ns Ar pid Ns Pa .sock
An editor's, which
.Nm
calls.
.It Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/arno- Ns Ar pid Ns /
The images C-v took off the clipboard, until the session ends; in
.Pa /tmp
when
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset.
.It Pa AGENTS.md , CLAUDE.md
Context files.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Ask one question and print the answer:
.Pp
.Dl $ arno 'where is Match defined?'
.Pp
Go on with the conversation that turn saved:
.Pp
.Dl $ arno \-\-resume 1 'now fix it'
.Pp
Use Claude, with a lower temperature and a test command, in
.Pa ~/.config/arno/config :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
provider    = claude
model       = claude-opus-5-5
temperature = 0.3
test_cmd    = make test
.Ed
.Pp
Work on a copy of every project (the default), and let the model read manuals and
draw:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
safety = auto
run   = tiza arkusz man
trust = man
.Ed
.Pp
An MCP server, and a second model for workers:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mcp    = files npx files-server /tmp
worker = deepseek-v4.1-flash
.Ed
.Pp
Watch, from a second terminal, the arno working in this directory, or
list the sessions and pick one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ arno \-\-attach
$ arno \-\-attach \-l
$ arno \-\-attach 4242
.Ed
.Pp
Messages that attach context:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Why does this fail? @src/parse.raz:120-160
Any left? @src/
Look at @"my notes/todo.md"
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr arno-peer 5 ,
.Xr arno-serve 5 ,
.Xr dwa 1 ,
.Xr git 1 ,
.Xr git-worktree 1 ,
.Xr man 1 ,
.Xr tmux 1 ,
.Xr xclip 1
.Sh CAVEATS
While the stage is up,
.Ic check
and a trusted
.Ic run
program execute files in the copy, which the model has edited without
approval.
They run in the jail then, but the jail does not stop all, as below,
and a test that needs the network, or writes to a cache in your home,
fails there.
Look at the diff before you apply it: what it changes in a Makefile runs
outside the jail once applied.
.Pp
The jail confines its command with Linux's unprivileged namespaces, and
that has limits.
The command can read every file you can, but for a fixed list of places
where secrets live:
.Pa ~/.ssh , ~/.gnupg , ~/.password-store , ~/.aws , ~/.azure ,
.Pa ~/.kube , ~/.docker , ~/.claude , ~/.config/gcloud , ~/.config/gh ,
.Pa ~/.local/share/keyrings , ~/.mozilla , ~/.config/chromium ,
.Pa ~/.config/google-chrome , ~/.netrc , ~/.git-credentials ,
.Pa ~/.npmrc , ~/.pypirc
and
.Pa ~/.cargo/credentials.toml ;
a secret kept anywhere else is readable.
It can connect to a Unix socket outside
.Pa /tmp , /dev/shm
and
.Pa /run ,
which are its own.
It has no network, so installing packages fails; do that yourself before
staging.
.Pp
A language server
.Pf ( Cm lsp. Ns Ar ext )
reads the project, and some run what its configuration says, as a
tsconfig's plugins; while staged that is the model's copy, so it runs
in the jail, with the limits above.
Unlike a jailed command, it stays in the terminal's session, in a
process group of its own, so it could write to the terminal.
Safe, it runs outside the jail, on your own files.
.Pp
The stage's copy is removed when
.Nm
ends in the ordinary way, but not on SIGTERM or SIGHUP; the next stage
sweeps it.
.Pp
A turn stops at the next read of the reply, not in the middle of a wait
for the server.
.Pp
Copying needs a terminal that honours OSC 52; others ignore it silently.
.Pp
Widths are counted in characters, so East Asian wide characters and
combined emoji can misalign the screen.
